+# arXiv 1603.04180 — anchored loose Hamilton cycles
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| native domain | combinatorics |
+| object regime | finite discrete |
+| computational core | permutation |
+| certificate form | exact symbolic cyclic ordering |
+| intuition | invariant: constant third finite difference modulo a prime |
+| domain essentiality | native |
+| reduction | none |
+
+This module turns Bastos, Mota, Schacht, Schnitzer, and Schulenburg,
+[*Loose Hamiltonian cycles forced by large \((k-2)\)-degree — approximate
+version*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.04180), into a witness problem.  The solver
+receives a genuine 4-uniform hypergraph and must return a cyclic ordering whose
+four-vertex blocks, taken every three positions, are hyperedges.  The checker
+uses only exact set membership and cyclic indexing.
+
+Trust status: all local gates G1–G9(c) pass, including 12/12 planted checks,
+five distinct corruption failures, zero valid answers in 200,000
+structure-aware guesses, four attacks at 0/8, and 60/60 canonical-key
+relabelling checks.  The mandatory four-vendor oracle evidence is **not yet
+available**: on 2026-09-05 `harden.py` received HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded
+(total limit)” on every retry and correctly aborted.  The error-only
+script-owned transcripts are retained; they are not counted as model failures
+and this result is not submission-ready until the harness is rerun with quota.
+
+## Construction and hardness claim
+
+Section 1 supplies the exact definition.  For `k=4, ell=1`, `n` must be a
+multiple of three.  Theorem 3 says sufficiently large hypergraphs with
+minimum 2-degree at least \((11/36+\gamma)\binom n2\) contain such a cycle.
+Section 2.1 proves existence through absorption, a reservoir, and path tiling.
+It does not establish computational or average-case hardness, so this module
+does **not** make a Track A claim and does not claim to sample the dense
+Theorem 3 regime.
+
+Instead, inverse generation chooses a random loose Hamilton cycle first.  It
+adds random loose-cycle layers in triples arranged so every vertex has exactly
+the same degree, then shuffles all edges.  A relabelling makes the planted
+ordering follow a constant-third-difference recurrence modulo 1,000,003.  The
+certificate is therefore known without solving the completed instance.
+
+Track B is explicit about both routes.  Generic anchored fail-first DFS is
+exponential in the worst case; on eight shipping instances it solved 8/8 at a
+median 146,921 nodes and 1.225 seconds.  The construction-specific algorithm
+computes three initial finite-difference levels, advances the recurrence, and
+checks the resulting blocks in `O(n+m)`: 114 exact operations and a median
+0.000010 seconds.  That 114-operation route is below the no-tool cap but is not
+apparent from ordinary hypergraph search.
+
+## Worked demo (`n=9`, `layers=3`, `seed=7`)
+
+The complete rendered instance is:
+
+```text
+ANCHORED LOOSE HAMILTON CYCLE IN A 4-UNIFORM HYPERGRAPH
+
+A 4-uniform hypergraph consists of a vertex set and hyperedges, each hyperedge
+being an unordered set of exactly four distinct vertices.  A Hamilton 1-cycle is
+a cyclic ordering x[0],...,x[n-1] of every vertex exactly once such that, for
+each i=0,3,6,...,n-3, the unordered set
+
+  {x[i], x[(i+1) mod n], x[(i+2) mod n], x[(i+3) mod n]}
+
+is a displayed hyperedge.  Thus consecutive cycle edges share exactly the one
+vertex at their boundary.  Here n=9, so the cycle has 3 hyperedges.  Indices in
+this definition are 0-based and all modular indexing of x uses modulus n.
+Vertex labels themselves are ordinary exact integers in the range 0 through
+1000002; 1000003 is also displayed as an instance modulus.  Hyperedge order and
+the order of labels inside an edge carry no meaning.
+
+To remove cyclic rotation and orientation ambiguity, your ordering must begin
+with this exact four-vertex prefix:
+
+  605136 613984 415949 51998
+
+The complete vertex set is:
+
+  51998 177357 374325 415949 563101 605136 613984 756383 990222
+
+The 9 hyperedges are:
+
+  0: 51998 415949 756383 990222
+  1: 177357 374325 563101 756383
+  2: 51998 177357 415949 990222
+  3: 415949 563101 605136 613984
+  4: 177357 374325 605136 756383
+  5: 51998 374325 563101 990222
+  6: 177357 563101 613984 990222
+  7: 51998 415949 605136 613984
+  8: 374325 605136 613984 756383
+
+Return exactly 9 comma-separated integer labels: the anchored cyclic ordering,
+containing each displayed vertex exactly once and no other label.  Give your
+final answer inside <answer></answer> tags.
+Syntax-only example: <answer>17, 4, 29</answer>
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+The answer is
+`[605136, 613984, 415949, 51998, 563101, 990222, 374325, 756383, 177357]`.
+`verify` returns `(True, "ok")`.  Swapping positions 4 and 6 gives
+`[605136, 613984, 415949, 51998, 374325, 990222, 563101, 756383, 177357]`;
+verification returns `(False, "cyclic block starting at position 6 is not a
+hyperedge")`.  A person can solve this smallest case on paper by extending the
+third finite difference five times, though the modular arithmetic is deliberate.
+
