codex
Implemented a native Track B generator for the paper’s 4-uniform loose Hamilton-cycle problem.

Files:

- [gen_1603_04180.py](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1603.04180/gen_1603_04180.py)
- [selftest_report.json](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1603.04180/selftest_report.json)
- [README.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1603.04180/README.md)
- [bare transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1603.04180/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [hinted transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1603.04180/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1603.04180/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

All local gates pass. Highlights:

- 12/12 planted witnesses verified.
- 0/200,000 structure-aware random guesses succeeded.
- Four attacks each failed on 8/8 instances.
- Generic DFS: median 146,921 nodes.
- Compact recurrence: 114 exact operations.
- 60/60 relabelling-invariance checks passed.
- Answer size: 289 characters, 36 elements.

The family uses the paper’s native loose-cycle definition but makes no false claim that its asymptotic degree theorem proves computational hardness. See the [paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.04180).

The result is not submission-ready because OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded” for every bare, hinted, and placebo attempt. The preserved transcripts contain only script-generated error records; no oracle failure has been counted as hardness evidence. Restore the OpenRouter quota and rerun the three harness arms to complete STEP 4.
